OrangePi RV2 (Armbian, SpacemiT K1 / Ky X1) - troubleshooting log
================================================================
Laptop: NixOS, serial console on /dev/ttyUSB0 @ 115200 8N1.
Board: OrangePi RV2, 4 GiB RAM, booting from 64 GB microSD (mmcblk0),
       plus 128 GB NVMe mounted at /mnt/ssd (only app configs/personal data).
Power: USB-C phone charger (model/rating to be confirmed), small heatsink.
Known: reboot via software does not work; cold boot after unplugging 5+ min
       sometimes recovers (capacitor-discharge theory from hydrogen18 blog).

------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-11-21 - Session 1 - initial diagnosis (READ ONLY so far)
------------------------------------------------------------------
State when started: board stuck at initramfs rescue prompt. Serial log
(orangepi-booting-log-trough-serial) shows:
  - SPL/U-Boot/DDR/kernel load all succeed, boot from mmc 0:1.
  - U-Boot: "*** Warning - bad CRC, using default environment"
  - U-Boot: "MAC mapping file path not set in environment"
  - initramfs: "Timed out while waiting for udev queue to empty" x3
  - root /dev/mmcblk0p1 (ext4 label armbi_root) fsck OK, journal recovered
  - run-init: "opening console: No such file or directory"
  - run-init: "Target filesystem doesn't have requested /sbin/init."
  - "No init found. Try passing init= bootarg."
 => failure is a ROOT FILESYSTEM problem, not bootloader/power, this time.

No changes made to the board yet.
Serial access method: user chowns /dev/ttyUSB0 to user (one-time, per boot).
Helper: ./serial.sh (stty raw + cat capture) - host-side only.

Follow-up (read-only) findings from the live initramfs shell:
  - Board is ALIVE, kernel 6.18.55-current-spacemit (Armbian 26.11 trunk, Debian 13).
  - Root /dev/mmcblk0p1 (ext4, label armbi_root) mounts fine, /sbin/init exists,
    and `chroot /root /sbin/init` actually executes systemd 257. Rootfs is OK.
  - Real failure: /usr/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/init-bottom/udev is
    `#!/bin/sh -e` and starts with `udevadm settle --timeout=60`.
    At boot, udev never empties its queue within 60s, so the script aborts
    BEFORE `mount --move /dev /root/dev`.
    Result: /root/dev is empty -> /root/dev/console missing -> run-init fails
    ("Target filesystem doesn't have requested /sbin/init", "No init found").
  - udev timeline from /run/udev/data timestamps: 290 of 293 device records
    are processed within ~12s; only b179:1 (mmcblk0p1) and c10:234
    (/dev/btrfs-control, appears when btrfs module loads) are processed at
    ~121s and ~131s. Root is mounted at t=135s.
  - Three `udevadm settle` timeouts per boot (~60s each) explain the ~4 min
    boot: init-top/udev (bare `udevadm settle || true`), a `wait_for_udev`
    call with no timeout, and init-bottom/udev (fatal, the boot killer).
  - Root UUID is resolved with blkid (direct device scan) plus
    `wait_for_udev 10`; it does NOT depend on udev by-uuid symlinks, and the
    SD card/partition is registered at t=3.2s. So shortening the udev waits
    is safe on this board.
  - U-Boot still reports "bad CRC, using default environment" and
    "MAC mapping file path not set" (separate issue, not fixed yet).

Planned change (awaiting user consent):
  1. Patch (with .pre-udevfix backups) three initramfs source scripts:
       scripts/functions      : settle timeout -> 20s, never fails
       scripts/init-top/udev  : settle timeout -> 20s, never fails
       scripts/init-bottom/udev: settle timeout -> 180s, never fails (|| true)
  2. Apply the same patch to the *current* initrd by extract/patch/repack:
       /boot/initrd.img-6.18.55-current-spacemit
       /boot/uInitrd-6.18.55-current-spacemit (rebuild with mkimage -A riscv)
     with .pre-udevfix backups, and verify by diffing extracted trees.
  3. Temporarily set loglevel=6 in /boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf for the next
     boot (backup kept) so the udev/boot timeline is visible.
  4. Requires a manual power cycle (software reboot is broken on this board).

------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-11-21 ~03:40-03:46 UTC - change executed
------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Patch script /var/tmp/fix-initrd.sh written on the board (sha256 e0bf9f14...).
  * Three source scripts patched (backups *.pre-udevfix):
      /usr/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/functions
      /usr/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/init-top/udev
      /usr/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/init-bottom/udev
    New content:
      functions:        udevadm settle --timeout="${1:-20}" || true
      init-top/udev:    udevadm settle --timeout=20 || true
      init-bottom/udev: udevadm settle --timeout=180 || true
  * First manual-repack attempt aborted safely: initrd.img is NOT gzip; it is a
    concatenation of cpio archives (raw cpio magic 070701, cpio -t stops after
    1.9 MB / 166 entries). No boot files were modified by that attempt.
  * Used the proper tool instead: `update-initramfs -u -k 6.18.55-current-spacemit`
    (run chrooted from the initramfs shell; /dev,/proc,/sys bind-mounted then
    unmounted). It regenerated initrd.img + uInitrd via Armbian's 99-uboot hook.
  * Backups (original, verified sha256):
      /boot/initrd.img-6.18.55-current-spacemit.pre-udevfix  ab1f5cef...
      /boot/uInitrd-6.18.55-current-spacemit.pre-udevfix     65d83c4e...
      /boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf.pre-udevfix
  * New files:
      initrd.img sha256 16b3288a... ; uInitrd data sha256 identical
  * Verified with unmkinitramfs that the new initramfs contains the patched
     hooks (and harmless *.pre-udevfix copies inside the initramfs).
  * /boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf append changed loglevel=1 -> loglevel=6
    (temporary, for diagnosis).
  * Power cycle requested (unplug ~20s) with boot captured to boot-new.log.

RESULT: BOOT SUCCESSFUL. Board reached multi-user, SSH on 192.168.x.x works,
all services started (uptime 5 min). Rootfs remounted rw, no MMC/EXT4 errors.

Problem introduced by the patch: backups named `*.pre-udevfix` were left inside
the scanned initramfs script directories and are executable, so run_scripts also
executed them:
  - /usr/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/init-top/udev.pre-udevfix
  - /usr/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/init-bottom/udev.pre-udevfix
Effect: a second systemd-udevd was started in the initramfs and two udevds are
now running (PID 193 initramfs + PID 388 systemd). Boot still succeeded but took
~3.6 min (root mounted at t=156s). Fix: move those backups out of the scanned
directories, regenerate initramfs, kill the stray udevd.

Watchdog findings on the running system (kernel 6.18.55 mainline-ish):
  - CONFIG_SPACEMIT_WATCHDOG=y (NOT the old buggy CONFIG_KY_WATCHDOG from the blog)
  - /dev/watchdog, /dev/watchdog0 exist; driver logs "System boots up not
    because of SoC watchdog reset."
  - CONFIG_WATCHDOG_SYSFS is not set, so /sys/class/watchdog/watchdog0 has no
    identity/timeout/state attributes; ioctl use via /dev/watchdog still works.
  - DT has watchdog@d4080000, no "spa,wdt-enable-restart-handler" property.
  - systemd: RuntimeWatchdogUSec=0 (off), RebootWatchdogUSec=10min.
  Reboot behaviour still untested on this kernel.

------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-11-21 ~01:15-01:30 - reboot / SD-card wedge test
------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Enabled the hardware watchdog in systemd for reboot safety:
      /etc/systemd/system.conf.d/10-watchdog.conf
        RuntimeWatchdogSec=60s
        RebootWatchdogSec=15s
        ShutdownWatchdogSec=15s
    systemd logs: "Using hardware watchdog 'K1X Watchdog', version 0,
    device /dev/watchdog0", RuntimeWatchdogUSec=1min, PID 1 holds it.
  * `sudo reboot` result:
      - systemd reached reboot.target, kernel printed "reboot: Restarting system".
      - SoC reset, U-Boot SPL ran, but the SD card was WEDGED:
          "mmc fail to set block count"
          "** fs_devread read error - block"
          "Failed to mount ext2 filesystem..."
          "Skipping Armbian_community for failure retrieving initrd"
          "** No partition table - mmc 0 **"
      - U-Boot then tried mmc 1:1 and mmc 2:1 (NVMe) and dropped to `=>`.
  * At the U-Boot prompt the card answers `mmc info` (SE64G, 59.5 GiB) but every
    `mmc read` returns "0 blocks read: ERROR", even for the MBR sector and the
    first partition sector. `mmc part` only shows a cached table.
    `mmc rescan`, `mmc dev 0`, and speed changes do NOT recover data reads.
  * `poweroff` at the U-Boot prompt + power-button start produced the SAME wedge
    on the next cold boot (SPL ran, U-Boot still couldn't read mmc 0).
  * Conclusion: a warm SoC reset (and even a short button power cycle) leaves the
    SD card/controller unable to do data transfers. Only a real removal of power
    for minutes restores it. This is the hardware limitation from the blog, and
    it is independent of the initramfs fix.
  * systemd watchdog does reset the SoC, but lands in U-Boot with the wedged card,
    so it is NOT a reboot fix.

  * Kernel driver audit (Armbian 6.18.55-current-spacemit):
      - Driver is vendor drivers/watchdog/k1x_wdt.c (symbols spa_wdt_*), not
        mainline; boot message "System boots up not because of SoC watchdog reset."
      - It still declares a global `static spinlock_t reboot_lock;` and only
        `spin_lock_init(&info->wdt_lock)` exists; reboot_lock is NOT initialized.
        => the blog's "BUG: spinlock bad magic" bug is still present in source.
      - DTB has `spa,wdt-disabled` but NOT `spa,wdt-enable-restart-handler`;
        therefore the restart handler is not registered (matches the hang).
      - The blog's kernel fixes are NOT in this kernel.

